/* Psych, Basic psychology. Original cards. */
(window.PA_CARDS = window.PA_CARDS || {}).psych = (window.PA_CARDS.psych || []).concat([
/* ---------- Perception ---------- */
["Perception","Gestalt principles","What is the central idea of Gestalt psychology about perception?","We perceive organised wholes, not separate parts","The brain groups raw sensory input into unified forms. The whole is seen as different from the sum of its parts."],
["Perception","Gestalt principles","Which Gestalt principle makes us see items placed near each other as one group?","Proximity","Things close together in space are read as belonging together."],
["Perception","Gestalt principles","Which Gestalt principle makes us see a circle with a small gap as a complete circle?","Closure","The brain fills in missing parts to complete a familiar shape."],
["Perception","Gestalt principles","Which Gestalt principle groups items that share colour, shape or size?","Similarity","Alike items are perceived as a set, even when mixed with others."],
["Perception","Gestalt principles","Which Gestalt principle makes us follow a smooth line through a crossing rather than see sharp turns?","Continuity (good continuation)","The visual system prefers smooth, continuous paths over abrupt changes in direction."],
["Perception","Gestalt principles","Name five Gestalt laws of perceptual organisation.","Proximity, similarity, closure, continuity and symmetry","These are innate grouping rules. Familiarity and past experience belong to top-down processing rather than the classic grouping laws."],
["Perception","Gestalt principles","What is figure-ground perception?","Separating an object (figure) from its background (ground)","Rubin's vase shows that figure and ground can flip, so the same image is seen as a vase or two faces."],
["Perception","Perceptual set and processing","What is a perceptual set?","A temporary readiness to perceive a stimulus in a particular way","Expectation primes us to notice some features. It is selective, so other features of the stimulus are missed."],
["Perception","Perceptual set and processing","Name four factors in the perceiver that shape perceptual set.","Expectation, past experience, motivation (drive state) and emotion","Culture and personal values also matter. Each biases which interpretation the brain chooses."],
["Perception","Perceptual set and processing","A 28-year-old woman with panic disorder in a GP waiting room notices every frowning face around her. What perceptual process is shown?","Emotion-driven perceptual set (attentional bias to threat)","Anxiety lowers the threshold for detecting threat-related cues, so they are picked out first."],
["Perception","Perceptual set and processing","Poorer children judged coins to be larger than richer children did. What does this show?","Needs and values shape perception","Bruner and Goodman showed that what a stimulus means to the perceiver alters how it is seen."],
["Perception","Perceptual set and processing","What is the difference between bottom-up and top-down processing?","Bottom-up is driven by sensory data; top-down is driven by knowledge and expectation","Normal perception mixes both. Top-down effects explain why context changes what we see."],
["Perception","Perceptual set and processing","Which theorist said perception is direct, using information in the light and 'affordances'?","James Gibson","Gibson's ecological theory is bottom-up: the environment supplies enough information without inference."],
["Perception","Perceptual set and processing","Which theorist described perception as building a hypothesis from stored knowledge?","Richard Gregory","Gregory's constructivist theory is top-down. Illusions occur when the brain's best guess is wrong."],
["Perception","Constancy, depth and illusions","What is perceptual constancy?","Seeing an object as stable in size, shape and colour despite a changing retinal image","A door seen at an angle still looks rectangular because the brain corrects for viewing conditions."],
["Perception","Constancy, depth and illusions","Name three monocular depth cues.","Linear perspective, relative size, interposition (also texture gradient, shading, motion parallax)","These work with one eye, which is why painters can show depth on a flat canvas."],
["Perception","Constancy, depth and illusions","Which experiment showed that crawling infants avoid an apparent drop?","The visual cliff (Gibson and Walk)","Infants of about 6 months would not crawl over the glass above the 'drop', showing depth perception by crawling age."],
["Perception","Psychophysics","State Weber's law.","The just noticeable difference is a constant fraction of the original stimulus","A heavier weight needs a bigger added weight before a change is noticed."],
["Perception","Perceptual development","Which sense is least developed at birth?","Vision","Newborn acuity is very poor (around 20/300) with a fixed focus near 20 to 25 cm, about the distance to a carer's face."],
/* ---------- Attention ---------- */
["Attention","Theories of attention","Which theory says we can attend to only one channel at a time, with an early filter based on physical features?","Broadbent's filter theory","Broadbent proposed a bottleneck before meaning is analysed. Unattended input is blocked at the filter."],
["Attention","Theories of attention","What is dichotic listening?","Playing different messages to each ear while the person repeats (shadows) one","Cherry used it to study what is noticed in the unattended ear. Physical changes are noticed; content mostly is not."],
["Attention","Theories of attention","Which theory says unattended messages are weakened rather than blocked?","Treisman's attenuation theory","Weakened input can still break through if it is important or primed, such as one's own name."],
["Attention","Theories of attention","Which theory says all input is analysed for meaning before selection happens?","Late selection theory (Deutsch and Deutsch)","Selection occurs at the response stage, not at the sensory stage."],
["Attention","Theories of attention","What is the cocktail party effect?","Noticing your own name in a conversation you were not attending to","It shows some unattended input is processed for meaning, which challenges a strict early filter."],
["Attention", "Theories of attention", "A 34-year-old woman working as a nurse on a busy acute ward hears her name spoken across the room while talking to a patient. Which theory of attention does this challenge?", "Broadbent's early filter theory", "The unattended speech was processed for meaning, which a strict early filter should prevent. It fits Treisman's attenuation model."],
["Attention","Theories of attention","Which model sees attention as a limited pool of mental effort shared across tasks?","Kahneman's capacity (resource) model","Tasks can run together if their combined demand stays within available capacity, which varies with arousal."],
["Attention","Types and clinical testing","Name four types of attention.","Selective, divided, sustained (vigilance) and alternating","Different tests target each type, and different disorders spare or impair them."],
["Attention","Types and clinical testing","What does the Stroop test measure?","Selective attention and inhibition of a habitual response","Naming the ink colour of a clashing colour word is slow because reading is automatic. It is linked to frontal function."],
["Attention","Types and clinical testing","What happens to performance during a long, dull monitoring task?","It declines within about 30 minutes (vigilance decrement)","Mackworth's clock test showed missed signals rise as sustained attention fades."],
["Attention","Types and clinical testing","A 79-year-old woman on a medical ward becomes confused overnight with a urinary infection. Which cognitive deficit is the core feature?","Impaired attention","Delirium is a disturbance of attention and awareness. Other cognitive problems follow from it."],
["Attention","Types and clinical testing","Name three bedside tests of attention.","Months of the year backwards, serial sevens, digit span","Each needs sustained focus. Months backwards is used in the 4AT for delirium."],
/* ---------- Learning theory ---------- */
["Learning theory","Classical conditioning basics","In classical conditioning, what does the organism learn?","An association between two stimuli","A neutral stimulus gains meaning by predicting an unconditioned stimulus. Pavlov first described it."],
["Learning theory","Classical conditioning basics","In Pavlov's experiments, what was the conditioned stimulus?","The bell","It caused salivation only after being paired with food. Before pairing it was neutral."],
["Learning theory","Classical conditioning basics","What kind of responses are typically learned by classical conditioning?","Involuntary, often autonomic responses","Salivation, heart rate change and fear are reflexes, not chosen actions. Operant learning shapes voluntary behaviour."],
["Learning theory","Classical conditioning basics","Which experiment showed fear could be conditioned in an infant?","Watson and Rayner's 'Little Albert' study","A white rat was paired with a loud noise until the rat alone caused fear, which then spread to other furry objects."],
["Learning theory","Timing of conditioning","What is forward (delayed) conditioning?","The CS starts before the UCS and stays on until the response appears","This is the standard and most effective arrangement because the CS predicts the UCS."],
["Learning theory","Timing of conditioning","What is trace conditioning?","The CS ends before the UCS begins","The animal must hold a memory trace across the gap, so learning is weaker than with delayed conditioning."],
["Learning theory","Timing of conditioning","What is backward conditioning?","The UCS is given before the CS","The CS predicts nothing, so little or no learning occurs. It is the weakest form."],
["Learning theory","Timing of conditioning","What CS-UCS interval gives the strongest conditioning?","About half a second or less","The longer the gap, the weaker the association, because the CS becomes a poorer predictor."],
["Learning theory","Conditioning phenomena","What is extinction in classical conditioning?","The CR fades when the CS is repeatedly given without the UCS","The CS no longer predicts anything. Exposure therapy uses this principle."],
["Learning theory", "Conditioning phenomena", "A 38-year-old woman fears the dentist after a painful extraction, but the fear fades over months of painless check-ups. What process explains this?", "Extinction", "The CS (dentist) is repeatedly met without the UCS (pain), so the conditioned fear weakens."],
["Learning theory","Conditioning phenomena","An extinguished response returns after a rest period. What is this called?","Spontaneous recovery","It shows extinction is new inhibitory learning, not erasure of the original memory."],
["Learning theory", "Conditioning phenomena", "A 27-year-old man whose fear of lifts had faded feels anxious again in a lift after a year of not using one. What is this?", "Spontaneous recovery", "The original fear learning was not erased by extinction, so it can re-emerge after time away."],
["Learning theory","Conditioning phenomena","A 31-year-old woman bitten by a spider now fears beetles and moths too. What process explains this?","Stimulus generalisation","The conditioned response spreads to stimuli that resemble the original CS."],
["Learning theory","Conditioning phenomena","What is stimulus discrimination?","Responding to the CS but not to similar stimuli","It develops when only the true CS is followed by the UCS."],
["Learning theory","Conditioning phenomena","What is higher-order conditioning?","A new neutral stimulus is paired with an established CS, which acts as the UCS","Learning is weaker than first-order conditioning and extinguishes more easily."],
["Learning theory","Conditioning phenomena","What is incubation in conditioning?","A conditioned response grows stronger after brief, repeated CS exposure without the UCS","Eysenck used it to explain why short exposures can worsen phobias instead of curing them."],
["Learning theory", "Conditioning phenomena", "A 30-year-old man who has never been harmed by a snake has a severe snake phobia. Which concept explains why snakes are such common phobic objects?", "Preparedness (Seligman)", "We are biologically primed to fear threats from our evolutionary past. Such fears form quickly and resist extinction."],
["Learning theory","Conditioning phenomena","What is conditioned taste aversion?","Avoiding a taste after one pairing with nausea, even with hours between them","Garcia showed it breaks the usual short-interval rule, reflecting biological preparedness."],
["Learning theory","Conditioning phenomena","What is Kamin's blocking effect?","An existing CS prevents learning about a new CS added alongside it","The new stimulus adds no predictive information, so no association forms."],
["Learning theory","Conditioning phenomena","What is latent inhibition?","Prior exposure to a stimulus without consequence slows later conditioning to it","It filters out irrelevant stimuli. It is reduced in acute schizophrenia, linked to aberrant salience."],
["Learning theory","Conditioning phenomena","A 21-year-old medical student feels less disgust in the dissection room after several weeks. What process explains this?","Habituation","Repeated harmless exposure reduces the emotional response."],
["Learning theory","Operant conditioning","In operant conditioning, what is associated with what?","A voluntary response with its consequence","Behaviour is shaped by what follows it. Skinner studied this using the Skinner box."],
["Learning theory","Operant conditioning","What is Thorndike's law of effect?","Responses followed by satisfying outcomes are strengthened","His puzzle box cats escaped faster over trials. This laid the basis for Skinner's work."],
["Learning theory","Operant conditioning","What is positive reinforcement?","Adding a pleasant stimulus after a behaviour, making it more likely","Reinforcement always increases behaviour. 'Positive' means something is added."],
["Learning theory","Operant conditioning","What is negative reinforcement?","Removing an unpleasant stimulus after a behaviour, making it more likely","It increases behaviour. It is often confused with punishment, which decreases behaviour."],
["Learning theory","Operant conditioning","What is punishment in operant terms?","A consequence that makes a behaviour less likely","Positive punishment adds something unpleasant. Negative punishment removes something pleasant."],
["Learning theory","Operant conditioning","A 26-year-old man with OCD feels brief relief each time he checks the door locks. Which learning principle keeps the checking going?","Negative reinforcement","The ritual removes anxiety, so the ritual is strengthened."],
["Learning theory","Operant conditioning","A rat gets an electric shock each time it presses a lever and stops pressing. What has occurred?","Punishment (positive punishment)","An aversive consequence was added and the behaviour fell."],
["Learning theory", "Operant conditioning", "A 6-year-old girl's tantrums get worse for a few days after her parents start ignoring them. What is this?", "An extinction burst", "When reinforcement (attention) is withdrawn, behaviour briefly rises before it fades. Parents should be warned and stay consistent."],
["Learning theory","Operant conditioning","What is shaping?","Reinforcing successive approximations to a target behaviour","Complex behaviours that never occur by chance can be built step by step."],
["Learning theory","Operant conditioning","A rat is rewarded for pressing a lever only when a red light is on. What is the red light?","A discriminative stimulus","It signals when the response will be reinforced, so the rat learns to press only in its presence."],
["Learning theory","Operant conditioning","What is the difference between a primary and a secondary reinforcer?","Primary meets a biological need; secondary gains value through learning","Food is primary. Money and tokens are secondary because they are linked to primary rewards."],
["Learning theory","Operant conditioning","A mother tells her 8-year-old son he can play football only after his homework is done. Which principle is this?","The Premack principle","A more likely behaviour (play) is used to reinforce a less likely one (homework)."],
["Learning theory","Operant conditioning","What is a token economy?","Tokens earned for target behaviours are exchanged for rewards","It uses secondary reinforcers and has been used on long-stay and learning disability units."],
["Learning theory","Operant conditioning","What is time out aimed at?","Reducing unwanted behaviour","The person is briefly removed from all reinforcement, which is a form of negative punishment."],
["Learning theory","Schedules of reinforcement","How does continuous reinforcement affect learning and extinction?","Fastest learning but fastest extinction","Once rewards stop, the change is obvious, so the behaviour stops quickly."],
["Learning theory","Schedules of reinforcement","How does partial (intermittent) reinforcement affect learning and extinction?","Slower learning but much greater resistance to extinction","The person cannot tell when rewards have stopped, so they keep responding."],
["Learning theory","Schedules of reinforcement","Which schedule gives the highest response rate and greatest resistance to extinction?","Variable ratio","Any response might be the rewarded one. This drives gambling."],
["Learning theory","Schedules of reinforcement","A 59-year-old man keeps buying lottery tickets after a few small wins at random. Which reinforcement schedule is at work?","Variable ratio","Wins follow an unpredictable number of plays, producing persistent behaviour."],
["Learning theory","Schedules of reinforcement","What response pattern does a fixed interval schedule produce?","A scalloped pattern: little response after reward, rising before the next one","Students who cram just before a termly exam show this pattern."],
["Learning theory","Learned helplessness","Who described learned helplessness and which disorder does it model?","Seligman; depression","Dogs given inescapable shocks later failed to escape when escape was possible. Passivity mirrors depressive hopelessness."],
["Learning theory","Learned helplessness","In the reformulated learned helplessness model, which attributional style predicts depression?","Internal, stable and global attributions for bad events","Abramson and colleagues added cognition: 'It is my fault, it will last, it affects everything'."],
["Learning theory","Observational and cognitive learning","Who developed social learning theory?","Albert Bandura","He showed behaviour is learned by watching others. Modelling is linked to Bandura, not Skinner."],
["Learning theory","Observational and cognitive learning","What did the Bobo doll experiment show?","Children imitate aggression they have watched an adult perform","Learning occurred without the children being reinforced themselves."],
["Learning theory","Observational and cognitive learning","Name the four processes needed for observational learning.","Attention, retention, reproduction and motivation","Judgement is not one of them. The observer must notice, remember, be able to copy, and want to."],
["Learning theory","Observational and cognitive learning","Which features of a model make imitation more likely?","Similarity to the observer, high status, competence and power","People copy those they identify with or admire."],
["Learning theory","Behaviour therapy applications","What is reciprocal inhibition?","Two opposing states, such as anxiety and relaxation, cannot exist together","Wolpe used it as the basis of systematic desensitisation."],
["Learning theory","Behaviour therapy applications","A 36-year-old man with a phobia of escalators learns muscle relaxation, then imagines scenes from least to most feared. What is this treatment?","Systematic desensitisation","Relaxation is paired with a graded hierarchy so anxiety is inhibited at each step."],
["Learning theory","Behaviour therapy applications","A 4-year-old boy afraid of the family dog will stay near it only on his mother's lap while eating ice cream. Which principle is used?","Reciprocal inhibition (counterconditioning)","Comfort and pleasure compete with fear and weaken the fear response."],
["Learning theory", "Behaviour therapy applications", "A 32-year-old woman with a fear of pigeons stays in a square full of pigeons for two hours until her anxiety falls. What is this technique?", "Flooding", "Prolonged real-life exposure to the most feared stimulus without escape lets anxiety peak and then extinguish."],
["Learning theory","Behaviour therapy applications","What is implosion?","Flooding carried out in imagination","The patient imagines the top of the hierarchy at once, without gradual steps."],
["Learning theory","Behaviour therapy applications","A 29-year-old woman with contamination fears touches a bin lid and is helped not to wash her hands. What is this treatment?","Exposure and response prevention","Blocking the ritual lets anxiety fall naturally, ending the negative reinforcement loop."],
["Learning theory","Behaviour therapy applications","A 16-year-old girl in CAMHS pulls out her hair when stressed. Which behavioural treatment is first line?","Habit reversal training","She learns awareness of the urge and a competing response that cannot be done at the same time."],
["Learning theory","Behaviour therapy applications","Which learning principle underlies disulfiram in alcohol dependence?","Aversive conditioning","Drinking on disulfiram causes flushing, nausea and palpitations, so drinking is linked with an unpleasant outcome."],
["Learning theory","Behaviour therapy applications","What is biofeedback?","Real-time feedback of a body signal so the person learns to control it","Heart rate, blood pressure and muscle tension can be lowered, mostly through learned relaxation."],
["Learning theory","Behaviour therapy applications","What is Mowrer's two-factor theory?","Fear is learned by classical conditioning and kept going by operant avoidance","Avoidance brings relief (negative reinforcement), so the fear is never tested and does not extinguish."],
["Learning theory","Behaviour therapy applications","Which type of conditioning helps explain both agoraphobic avoidance and obsessional rituals?","Avoidance conditioning","Avoiding the feared outcome brings relief, so the behaviour is self-reinforcing and persists."],
["Learning theory","Behaviour therapy applications","A 24-year-old woman has avoided cars since a minor collision, without PTSD symptoms. What is the most suitable treatment?","Behaviour therapy with graded exposure","This is a simple conditioned fear. Gradual exposure lets the fear extinguish."],
/* ---------- Memory ---------- */
["Memory","Memory stores","Who proposed the multi-store model of memory?","Atkinson and Shiffrin","Information flows from sensory memory to short-term memory to long-term memory."],
["Memory","Memory stores","What are the features of sensory memory?","Large capacity, unprocessed, and very short lived","Visual (iconic) traces last under about half a second. Most fade before they are processed."],
["Memory","Memory stores","What allows information to pass from sensory memory into short-term memory?","Attention","Only attended input is transferred. The rest decays almost at once."],
["Memory","Memory stores","What is the capacity of short-term memory?","About 7 plus or minus 2 items","Miller's 'magical number seven'. Chunking increases the amount held."],
["Memory","Memory stores","How long does short-term memory last without rehearsal?","About 15 to 30 seconds","Without rehearsal, recall falls steeply within this time."],
["Memory", "Memory stores", "A 58-year-old man is given letters to remember, then counts backwards in threes before recall. What is this task?", "The Brown-Peterson task", "Counting blocks rehearsal. Recall falls sharply by about 18 seconds, showing how short short-term memory is."],
["Memory","Memory stores","What is chunking?","Grouping separate items into larger meaningful units","A phone number held as three chunks fits easily in short-term memory."],
["Memory","Memory stores","What is the difference between maintenance and elaborative rehearsal?","Maintenance is simple repetition; elaborative links new material to meaning","Elaborative rehearsal moves information into long-term memory. Repetition only keeps it in short-term memory."],
["Memory","Memory stores","What does the levels of processing theory state?","Deeper (meaning-based) processing gives more lasting memories","Craik and Lockhart found semantic processing beats sound-based, which beats visual."],
["Memory","Working memory","Name the parts of Baddeley's working memory model.","Central executive, phonological loop, visuospatial sketchpad and episodic buffer","The executive controls two slave systems. The episodic buffer was added in 2000."],
["Memory","Working memory","Which brain area is most linked to working memory?","The dorsolateral prefrontal cortex","Working memory holds and manipulates information online, a frontal executive role."],
["Memory","Serial position effects","What is the primacy effect?","Better recall of the first items in a list","Early items get more rehearsal and enter long-term memory."],
["Memory","Serial position effects","A 66-year-old man leaving his GP appointment best remembers the first advice he was given. What effect is this?","The primacy effect","Early information was rehearsed and stored in long-term memory. Clinicians should give key advice first."],
["Memory","Serial position effects","Which serial position effect is lost if a distractor task is given before recall?","The recency effect","Recency depends on short-term memory, which is cleared by distraction. Primacy is unaffected."],
["Memory","Long-term memory systems","How does Squire divide long-term memory?","Declarative (explicit) and non-declarative (implicit)","Declarative memory can be consciously recalled. Non-declarative memory shows only in performance."],
["Memory","Long-term memory systems","What is episodic memory?","Memory for personal events tied to a time and place","Tulving described it. It is the system most damaged in amnesic syndrome."],
["Memory","Long-term memory systems","What is semantic memory?","Memory for facts and concepts without time or place of learning","Knowing that Edinburgh is the capital of Scotland uses semantic memory."],
["Memory","Long-term memory systems","Which type of memory cannot be consciously inspected?","Non-declarative (implicit) memory","It includes skills, priming and conditioning. It is shown by doing, not by recalling."],
["Memory","Long-term memory systems","What is procedural memory?","Memory for how to do skills, such as cycling or typing","It is non-declarative and relies on basal ganglia and cerebellum rather than the hippocampus."],
["Memory","Long-term memory systems","What is priming?","Faster or easier processing of a stimulus after prior exposure, without awareness","It is a form of implicit memory and is often preserved in amnesia."],
["Memory","Long-term memory systems","A 52-year-old woman means to take her tablets each evening but keeps forgetting. Which memory is failing?","Prospective memory","This is memory for future intentions, a common cause of everyday absent-mindedness."],
["Memory","Long-term memory systems","What are flashbulb memories?","Vivid, detailed memories of where one was when hearing emotional news","They feel certain but are often inaccurate. Confidence is not the same as accuracy."],
["Memory","Long-term memory systems","A 48-year-old man recalls exactly where he stood and what he was eating when he heard about the 9/11 attacks. What is this memory?","Flashbulb memory","Strong emotion and repeated retelling make it vivid, but details may still be wrong."],
["Memory","Encoding and retrieval","What is encoding?","Forming the initial memory trace","Information is turned into a form the brain can store."],
["Memory","Encoding and retrieval","Name three strategies that improve encoding.","Chunking, mnemonics and imagery (also organising and adding meaning)","Cueing helps retrieval rather than encoding."],
["Memory","Encoding and retrieval","What is the encoding specificity principle?","Recall is best when retrieval cues match those present at encoding","Tulving's principle underlies both context- and state-dependent memory."],
["Memory","Encoding and retrieval","Divers learned words underwater or on land. When was recall best?","In the same setting as learning","Godden and Baddeley showed context-dependent memory: the environment acts as a cue."],
["Memory","Encoding and retrieval","A 40-year-old man now depressed again recalls his last depressive episode clearly but his well periods poorly. What is this?","State-dependent memory","Material learned in one internal state is best recalled in the same state."],
["Memory","Encoding and retrieval","Answering a multiple choice exam uses which type of retrieval?","Recognition","The answer is presented and only needs to be identified, which is easier than free recall."],
["Memory","Encoding and retrieval","A 34-year-old woman with anxiety enters a church and feels it is familiar as stored memories come to mind. What retrieval process is this?","Recognition","A present stimulus is matched with a stored memory, giving a sense of familiarity."],
["Memory","Encoding and retrieval","What is reintegration?","Rebuilding a whole past experience from a small cue","Hearing the first line of a song and recalling the whole evening you first heard it is an example."],
["Memory","Encoding and retrieval","What is the tip of the tongue state an example of?","Blocking (temporary retrieval failure)","The memory is stored and partly available, such as its first letter, but cannot be fully retrieved."],
["Memory","Reconstruction and distortion","What did Bartlett's 'War of the Ghosts' study show?","Memory is reconstructive and shaped by schemas","Recall of an unfamiliar story drifted towards the reader's own cultural expectations."],
["Memory","Reconstruction and distortion","What is retrospective falsification?","Unconscious distortion of past memories to fit present feelings or needs","Positive parts may be enlarged and negative parts dropped, or the reverse in depression."],
["Memory","Forgetting","What did Ebbinghaus's forgetting curve show?","Forgetting is fastest soon after learning, then levels off","Most loss occurs in the first hours. Some material is kept for a long time and relearning is quicker (savings)."],
["Memory","Forgetting","What is the commonest cause of forgetting from long-term memory?","Retrieval failure","The memory is still stored but the right cue is missing. Cues can bring it back."],
["Memory","Forgetting","What is thought to be the main cause of forgetting in short-term memory?","Trace decay and displacement","Unrehearsed traces fade within seconds, and new items push old ones out of a small store."],
["Memory","Forgetting","What is proactive interference?","Old learning disrupts recall of new learning","Remembering an old PIN can block recall of a new one."],
["Memory","Forgetting","What is retroactive interference?","New learning disrupts recall of old learning","Learning Spanish may make earlier French vocabulary harder to recall."],
["Memory","Forgetting","Motivated forgetting is based on which psychoanalytic concept?","Repression","Freud proposed painful memories are kept out of awareness to reduce anxiety."],
["Memory","Amnesia","A 19-year-old man on ICU after a head injury cannot recall the weeks before the injury. What type of amnesia is this?","Retrograde amnesia","It is loss of memories formed before the injury. Anterograde amnesia is failure to form new ones."],
["Memory","Amnesia","What is Ribot's law?","In organic amnesia, recent memories are lost before remote ones","Older memories are more consolidated and so are more resistant to brain damage."],
["Memory","Amnesia","A 55-year-old man with heavy alcohol use cannot form new memories and has patchy older memories, but his digit span and skills are normal. What is the diagnosis?","Korsakoff syndrome","Thiamine deficiency damages the mammillary bodies and thalamus. Working and procedural memory are spared; confabulation may occur."],
["Memory","Amnesia","In the amnesic syndrome, which memory is most impaired and which is spared?","Episodic memory is impaired; immediate memory is spared","Digit span is normal, so attention is intact, but new events are not stored."],
["Memory","Amnesia","A 40-year-old man has hippocampal damage after a head injury. Which memory is most likely spared?","Procedural memory","Skills rely on the basal ganglia and cerebellum, not the hippocampus."],
["Memory","Amnesia","What did patient HM show after bilateral medial temporal lobe removal?","Profound anterograde amnesia with preserved skill learning","He improved at mirror drawing without remembering practising, showing declarative and procedural memory are separate."],
["Memory", "Amnesia", "A 20-year-old woman cannot recall anything from a party where she drank heavily, though friends say she chatted normally. What type of memory loss is this?", "Anterograde amnesia from failed consolidation (alcoholic blackout)", "Alcohol blocks transfer of new information into long-term memory, so events during intoxication are never stored."],
["Memory", "Amnesia", "A 63-year-old woman in A&E asks the same questions over and over for some hours after a cold sea swim, but knows who she is. What is the likely diagnosis?", "Transient global amnesia", "Sudden dense anterograde amnesia with intact identity, resolving within 24 hours. It is thought to involve temporary hippocampal dysfunction."],
["Memory","Amnesia","Name three settings where situation-specific amnesia is often reported.","Violent offences (especially homicide), PTSD and acute stress or dissociative states","Amnesia for an offence is claimed in a quarter or more of homicides. It is not typical of adjustment disorder."],
["Memory","Amnesia","Which measure of head injury severity best predicts outcome?","Duration of post-traumatic amnesia","Longer anterograde amnesia after the injury predicts worse cognitive and functional recovery."],
["Memory","Testing memory","A 45-year-old man on a brain injury unit has severe memory problems. Which bedside test assesses immediate memory?","Digit span","It tests immediate recall and attention. Forward span in adults is about 7 plus or minus 2."],
["Memory","Testing memory","What is a normal reverse digit span in a working-age adult?","About 5 plus or minus 2 digits","Reversing needs mental manipulation, so the span is shorter than forward span."],
["Memory","Testing memory","Which simple question tests recent memory at the bedside?","What the person ate at their last meal","It needs recall of a new personal event from hours ago and can be checked with staff."],
["Memory","Testing memory","A 71-year-old man in a memory clinic is given pairs of words, then asked for the second word of each pair. What does this test?","Verbal learning and memory (paired associate learning)","It measures the ability to form new verbal associations, which fails early in hippocampal disease."],
["Memory","Testing memory","Which bedside memory test is most sensitive to early age-related or Alzheimer memory decline?","Delayed recall","Items may be registered but are lost over minutes when consolidation fails."],
/* ---------- Intelligence ---------- */
["Intelligence","Theories of intelligence","What is Spearman's 'g'?","A general intelligence factor underlying all mental tests","Using factor analysis he found scores on different tests correlate, plus specific 's' factors for each test."],
["Intelligence","Theories of intelligence","What is the difference between fluid and crystallised intelligence?","Fluid is novel problem solving; crystallised is stored knowledge and vocabulary","Cattell and Horn's model. Fluid ability declines from early adulthood; crystallised ability is stable or rises."],
["Intelligence","Theories of intelligence","Which theory says intelligence combines analytical, creative and practical abilities?","Sternberg's triarchic theory","It stresses real-world adaptation, not just test performance."],
["Intelligence","Measuring intelligence","What is the Flynn effect?","Average IQ scores rise across generations","Gains of about 3 points per decade were seen in many countries, so tests need re-norming."],
["Intelligence","Measuring intelligence","What are the mean and standard deviation of IQ scores?","Mean 100, standard deviation 15","Modern tests use deviation IQ: performance compared with same-age peers."],
["Intelligence","Measuring intelligence","Who developed the first practical intelligence test and why?","Alfred Binet, to find French schoolchildren who needed extra help","The Binet-Simon test introduced mental age. Stern later proposed the IQ ratio."],
["Intelligence","Measuring intelligence","From what age is the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale used?","16 years and over","The WISC is used for children of school age; the WPPSI for younger children."],
["Intelligence", "Measuring intelligence", "A 9-year-old boy referred to CAMHS needs a full IQ assessment. Which Wechsler test is used?", "The Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children (WISC)", "The WISC covers school-age children up to 16; the WAIS takes over from 16."],
["Intelligence","Measuring intelligence","What does Raven's Progressive Matrices measure?","Non-verbal general cognitive ability (fluid reasoning)","Patterns with a missing piece reduce reliance on language and schooling."],
["Intelligence","Measuring intelligence","A 70-year-old retired miner in a memory clinic has cognitive decline. Which test estimates his premorbid IQ?","The National Adult Reading Test (NART)","It uses reading of irregular words, which is relatively preserved in early dementia."],
["Intelligence","Measuring intelligence","Why does the NART estimate premorbid ability?","Irregular words can only be read from prior knowledge, which resists early decline","Sounding out does not work, so correct reading reflects long-standing vocabulary."],
["Intelligence","Measuring intelligence","How is disorder of intellectual development defined in ICD-11?","Intellectual and adaptive functioning about 2 or more standard deviations below the mean, starting in development","This is roughly an IQ below 70. Adaptive deficits are needed, not a score alone."],
["Intelligence","Abstract reasoning tests","What do the Goldstein-Scheerer sorting tests assess?","Abstract thinking and concept formation","Sorting items by colour, form or material reveals concrete thinking after brain damage."],
["Intelligence","Abstract reasoning tests","What does the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test assess?","Set shifting and abstract reasoning","Perseverative errors after a rule change suggest dorsolateral prefrontal dysfunction."],
["Intelligence","Abstract reasoning tests","What is Piaget's basic unit of intelligent behaviour?","The schema","Schemas are mental frameworks that grow through assimilation and accommodation."],
/* ---------- Thought and language ---------- */
["Thought and language","Problem solving and reasoning","What is the difference between an algorithm and a heuristic?","An algorithm guarantees a solution; a heuristic is a quick rule of thumb","Heuristics save effort but can lead to systematic errors."],
["Thought and language","Problem solving and reasoning","What is the availability heuristic?","Judging how likely something is by how easily examples come to mind","After air crash news, people overestimate flying risk. Tversky and Kahneman described it."],
["Thought and language","Problem solving and reasoning","What is the representativeness heuristic?","Judging likelihood by how closely something matches a typical case","It leads people to ignore base rates."],
["Thought and language","Problem solving and reasoning","A 45-year-old man in a gambling clinic says a win is 'due' after a long run of losses. What error is this?","The gambler's fallacy","Each independent event has the same odds; past losses do not raise future chances."],
["Thought and language","Problem solving and reasoning","What is confirmation bias?","Seeking evidence that supports a belief and ignoring evidence against it","The Wason selection task shows people rarely look for disconfirming cases."],
["Thought and language","Cognitive distortions","A 22-year-old male student gets one exam question wrong and decides he will fail the whole exam. What distortion is this?","Overgeneralisation","A single negative event is taken as a rule for everything."],
["Thought and language", "Cognitive distortions", "A 41-year-old man in talking therapy is sure a colleague ignored him because he is boring, with no other evidence. What distortion is this?", "Arbitrary inference", "He reaches a negative conclusion without supporting evidence. Other explanations are not considered."],
["Thought and language", "Cognitive distortions", "A 30-year-old woman with depression seen by the CMHT says her presentation was a disaster because her boss queried one slide, ignoring all the praise. What distortion is this?", "Selective abstraction", "She focuses on one negative detail and ignores the wider, mostly positive context."],
["Thought and language","Cognitive distortions","What is personalisation?","Taking responsibility for negative events with no real link to oneself","A mother blames herself because it rained at her child's party."],
["Thought and language","Cognitive distortions","What is dichotomous thinking?","Seeing things in all-or-nothing terms","Anything short of perfect is seen as total failure."],
["Thought and language","Cognitive distortions","What is Beck's cognitive triad?","Negative views of the self, the world and the future","Beck saw these as the core of depressive thinking, maintained by distortions."],
["Thought and language","Language","What did Chomsky propose about language learning?","An innate language acquisition device and universal grammar","Children produce novel sentences they never heard, which reinforcement alone cannot explain."],
["Thought and language","Language","How did Skinner explain language learning?","Through operant reinforcement and imitation","Chomsky's critique argued this could not explain how fast children learn grammar."],
["Thought and language","Language","What is the Sapir-Whorf hypothesis?","Language shapes how we think and perceive","The strong form (language determines thought) is rejected; a weak influence has some support."],
["Thought and language","Language","A 68-year-old man after a stroke speaks fluently but makes no sense and cannot understand speech. What aphasia is this?","Wernicke's (receptive) aphasia","Damage to the posterior superior temporal gyrus impairs comprehension while fluency is kept."],
["Thought and language","Language","A 61-year-old woman after a stroke understands speech but speaks in slow, effortful phrases. What aphasia is this?","Broca's (expressive) aphasia","Damage to the inferior frontal gyrus impairs speech production; comprehension is mostly spared."],
/* ---------- Motivation ---------- */
["Motivation","Maslow's hierarchy","List Maslow's original five levels from bottom to top.","Physiological, safety, love and belonging, esteem, self-actualisation","Lower needs must be largely met before higher needs drive behaviour."],
["Motivation","Maslow's hierarchy","Which Maslow level covers self-respect, competence and recognition from others?","Esteem needs","They sit above love and belonging and below self-actualisation."],
["Motivation","Maslow's hierarchy","Which of Maslow's levels are deficiency needs?","The first four: physiological, safety, love and belonging, esteem","They drive behaviour when lacking. Self-actualisation is a growth need."],
["Motivation","Maslow's hierarchy","Where do cognitive and aesthetic needs sit in the expanded hierarchy?","Above esteem and below self-actualisation, as growth needs","They were added later, so cognitive needs follow, not precede, esteem needs."],
["Motivation","Theories of motivation","What is Hull's drive reduction theory?","Behaviour is driven by the need to reduce internal tension from unmet needs","Hunger creates a drive; eating reduces it and is reinforced."],
["Motivation","Theories of motivation","Which theorist described homeostatic drives?","Walter Cannon","The body acts to keep internal states stable, such as temperature and blood glucose."],
["Motivation","Theories of motivation","Which approach do needs theory and goal theory take to motivation?","A socio-psychological approach","They explain complex motives in social settings such as work. Drive and homeostatic theories are biological."],
["Motivation","Theories of motivation","What three needs did McClelland describe?","Achievement, affiliation and power","Their balance predicts work behaviour and leadership style."],
["Motivation", "Theories of motivation", "A 23-year-old man does well on easy questions when anxious but freezes on hard ones in his exam. Which law explains this?", "The Yerkes-Dodson law", "Performance follows an inverted U with arousal. The optimal arousal level is lower for complex tasks."],
["Motivation","Theories of motivation","What is the overjustification effect?","External rewards for an enjoyable task reduce intrinsic motivation","Children paid to draw drew less once rewards stopped."],
["Motivation","Theories of motivation","Which hypothalamic areas control feeding and satiety?","Lateral hypothalamus for feeding; ventromedial hypothalamus for satiety","Lateral lesions cause loss of eating; ventromedial lesions cause overeating and obesity in animals."],
/* ---------- Emotion ---------- */
["Emotion","Theories of emotion","What does the James-Lange theory state?","Body changes come first and the emotion is the perception of them","'We feel afraid because we tremble.'"],
["Emotion","Theories of emotion","What does the Cannon-Bard theory state?","Body arousal and emotion occur at the same time and independently","Cannon noted visceral changes are too slow and similar across emotions. He gave the thalamus a central role."],
["Emotion", "Theories of emotion", "A 25-year-old man is injected with adrenaline without being told its effects and becomes euphoric beside a cheerful stooge. Which theory does this support?", "Schachter and Singer's two-factor theory", "Emotion is arousal plus a cognitive label. With no explanation for the arousal, he labelled it from the social context."],
["Emotion","Theories of emotion","What is Lazarus's view of emotion?","Cognitive appraisal of a situation comes before and shapes the emotion","How we judge meaning and threat decides which emotion we feel."],
["Emotion","Basic emotions","Who proposed six universal basic emotions?","Paul Ekman","Happiness, sadness, fear, anger, surprise and disgust are recognised from facial expressions across cultures."],
["Emotion","Basic emotions","How many primary emotions did Plutchik propose and how are they arranged?","Eight, in four opposite pairs","Joy-sadness, trust-disgust, fear-anger, anticipation-surprise. Opposites cannot be felt at once."],
["Emotion","Basic emotions","According to Plutchik, how does love arise?","As a secondary emotion blending joy and trust (acceptance)","Secondary emotions are mixtures of primaries; optimism blends joy and anticipation."],
["Emotion","Basic emotions","Which emotion raised finger temperature most in Ekman's physiological studies?","Anger","Anger raised skin temperature, while fear raised heart rate but cooled the skin."],
["Emotion","Neural basis","Name the main structures of the Papez circuit.","Hippocampus, fornix, mammillary bodies, anterior thalamus and cingulate gyrus","Papez proposed it as the emotion circuit. It is now seen as more important for memory."],
["Emotion","Neural basis","Which structure is central to fear learning?","The amygdala","Bilateral damage removes fear responses, as in Kluver-Bucy syndrome."],
/* ---------- Stress and coping ---------- */
["Stress and coping","Models of stress","What are the three stages of Selye's general adaptation syndrome?","Alarm, resistance and exhaustion","Prolonged stress depletes resources and may lead to illness."],
["Stress and coping","Models of stress","In Lazarus and Folkman's model, what happens in primary appraisal?","The person judges whether the stressor is a threat, harm or challenge","Stress depends on the person's judgement, not just the event."],
["Stress and coping","Models of stress","In Lazarus and Folkman's model, what happens in secondary appraisal?","The person judges their resources and options for coping","Stress arises when demands seem to exceed resources."],
["Stress and coping","Models of stress","Which scale ranks life events by life change units?","The Social Readjustment Rating Scale (Holmes and Rahe)","Death of a spouse scores highest at 100. High totals were linked to later illness."],
["Stress and coping","Models of stress","Which may predict health better than major life events?","Daily hassles","Kanner and Lazarus found small repeated stresses add up and relate closely to symptoms."],
["Stress and coping","Models of stress","What are the three parts of hardiness?","Commitment, control and challenge","Kobasa found executives high in hardiness stayed healthier under stress."],
["Stress and coping","Coping","What is problem-focused coping?","Acting to change the stressful situation","It works best when the stressor can be controlled."],
["Stress and coping", "Coping", "A 39-year-old man who has been made redundant draws up a budget and applies for new jobs. What coping style is this?", "Problem-focused coping", "He acts directly on the stressor, which works best when the situation can be changed."],
["Stress and coping","Coping","What is emotion-focused coping?","Changing one's emotional response to a stressor","It suits stressors that cannot be changed, such as a bereavement."],
["Stress and coping","Coping","A 42-year-old woman whose husband has terminal cancer joins a support group and keeps a feelings diary. What coping style is this?","Emotion-focused coping","She cannot change the illness, so she manages her emotional reaction to it."],
["Stress and coping","Coping","Is there a single best coping strategy?","No","The best strategy depends on the stressor and how controllable it is. People use several strategies at once."],
["Stress and coping","Coping","A 47-year-old man feels his hands sweat and his heart race when his manager shouts. What response is this?","The fight or flight response","Cannon described sympathetic and adrenal activation that prepares the body for action."],
/* ---------- Personality ---------- */
["Personality","Type A and B","Name the core features of Type A personality.","Competitiveness, impatience, time urgency and hostility","Friedman and Rosenman linked it to coronary heart disease. Hostility is the most toxic part."],
["Personality","Type A and B","How do Type B people handle stress?","They are relaxed and avoid stress but cope poorly when stress cannot be avoided","Their easy-going style is the opposite of the driven Type A pattern."],
["Personality","Type A and B","A 50-year-old man on a cardiac ward after a heart attack is hostile, impatient and works 80-hour weeks. Which personality pattern is this?","Type A behaviour pattern","Its hostility component is the part most strongly linked to coronary disease."],
["Personality","Trait theories","Name the Big Five personality traits.","Openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness, neuroticism (OCEAN)","Costa and McCrae's model is measured by the NEO-PI and is stable across cultures."],
["Personality","Trait theories","Which Big Five trait describes being tense, moody and anxious?","Neuroticism","High neuroticism predicts anxiety and depressive disorders."],
["Personality","Trait theories","Which Big Five trait describes being organised, thorough and dependable?","Conscientiousness","It predicts work performance and health behaviours."],
["Personality","Trait theories","Name Eysenck's three dimensions of personality.","Psychoticism, extraversion and neuroticism (PEN)","Psychoticism is Eysenck's dimension and is not part of the Big Five."],
["Personality","Trait theories","Which questionnaire measures extraversion-introversion and neuroticism-stability?","The Eysenck Personality Inventory (EPI)","The later Eysenck Personality Questionnaire added psychoticism."],
["Personality","Trait theories","How did Eysenck explain extraversion biologically?","Extraverts have low baseline cortical arousal and seek stimulation","He linked this to the ascending reticular activating system."],
["Personality","Trait theories","Who developed the 16 personality factor questionnaire (16PF)?","Raymond Cattell","He used factor analysis to find 16 source traits beneath surface behaviour."],
["Personality","Trait theories","Which temperament dimensions are in Cloninger's model?","Novelty seeking, harm avoidance, reward dependence and persistence","He linked them to dopamine, serotonin and noradrenaline. Character dimensions are learned."],
["Personality","Other theories","What is Rotter's locus of control?","Belief that outcomes are controlled by oneself (internal) or by outside forces (external)","An external locus is linked to helplessness and depression."],
["Personality","Other theories","What is central to Carl Rogers' theory of personality?","The self-concept and the need for unconditional positive regard","Incongruence between self-concept and experience causes distress."],
["Personality","Personality assessment","Name three projective tests of personality.","Rorschach inkblot test, Thematic Apperception Test and sentence completion","Ambiguous stimuli invite the person to project inner needs and conflicts."],
["Personality","Personality assessment","Which is the least structured personality test?","The Rorschach inkblot test","Inkblots have no set meaning, so responses are almost entirely shaped by the person."],
["Personality", "Personality assessment", "A 26-year-old man in a psychology clinic is asked to make up stories about pictures of people in unclear scenes. Which test is this?", "The Thematic Apperception Test", "Murray designed it so the stories reveal the person's own needs and motives."],
["Personality","Personality assessment","What type of test is the MMPI?","An objective self-report questionnaire with validity scales","True or false answers are scored on clinical scales; validity scales detect faking or careless answers."]
]);
